ope on stdout\n  --root <dir>    read another checkout\n  --limit N       bound a listing\n  --after <id>    continue a listing after one identity\n  --agent <agent> filter run list by agent\n  --exit <code>   filter run list by exit code\n  --since <time>  filter run list by RFC3339 start time\n  --rescan        re-run the Auditor before reporting (audit show)\n  --follow        stream a Run log until it completes; excludes --json (run logs)\n  --rejected S    fixer review-request: the rejected SHA that must still be the branch tip\n  exit: 0 ok, 1 no work, 2 error, 4 not found, 5 conflict, 6 invalid arg\n        run logs --follow instead exits with the followed Run's own code\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788550640027},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I should not run `forest poll verifier` as that may dispatch a new run. It seems already running (me). Let me not interfere.\n\nNow, decision: pick one of the two descendant candidates. Both are test-only additions. Let me look at the diffs to understand and pick. Actually the instructions don't specify which one; I can pick either. But maybe there's a preferred order - process oldest first. The request times: 674894f9 at 19:30:31Z (older), 3a4a2ee4 at 19:34:52Z (newer). \n\nActually wait - there may be additional newer requests with no verdict that are stale (not descendants). The Kernel merge loop processes one at a time. Since 3a4a2ee4 and 674894f9 are both descendants, both can be reviewed. But note: after approving one, origin/master fast-forwards, which might make the other still a descendant (since both are based on current master but are independent branches). Actually both are descendants of c203dc91. If I approve 674894f9 first, master becomes 674894f9. Then 3a4a2ee4 might not contain 674894f9 (they're independent test-only branches), so 3a4a2ee4 would become stale! That's a concern: only one of them can be merged if they diverge.\n\nWait, let me think. Both branches are tes